WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.130
- All hour we are celebrating women

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.720
knocking down gender barriers in sports.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.340
My next guest is certainly kicking

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.250
aside any hurdles in her way.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.040
She's a two time all star

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.270
on the LA Sparks and she's
an NBA analyst for ESPN.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.256
Please, everybody welcome Chiney Ogwumike.

00:00:17.256 --> 00:00:22.256
(upbeat music plays)
(applause)

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:29.650
- You look so cute.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:31.373
I wish we could hug.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.272
And I wish I were tall.

00:00:34.272 --> 00:00:35.105
(Chiney laughs)

00:00:35.105 --> 00:00:36.840
- Oh, I'm five three it's so bad.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.900
- I could throw you an inch or two.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.570
- I was like, I'll take more than that.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:43.000
So you basically have two full-time jobs.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.350
I know how that feels.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.540
And yeah, so how do you deal with it?

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:47.570
How do you balance?

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:49.080
- I don't, I really don't.
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.303
- I am horrible

00:00:50.303 --> 00:00:51.810
- The honest answer folks.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.210
- I'm really horrible at balance,

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.720
but I have a great team,
family, friends, agent

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.350
that really keeps me on point.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:58.730
But I try my best to try to like

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.440
make sure everyone's proud with what,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.140
you know, what I'm doing on
the court, off the court.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.620
But it's hard to keep it all together,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.330
but you take it one day at a time.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.610
- I love that you're being
honest with it though.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:08.810
Cause I love what I do too.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.900
And I love, I love all
the jobs that I get to do,

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.450
but it is a very honest thing.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:13.430
Like some days you have days

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:14.970
where you're just like,
(mimics explosion sound)

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.320
- Hibernate
- And tears.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.669
- Yes

00:01:17.669 --> 00:01:19.550
- That's normal for anyone even with

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.210
you know, one job, but
you have some pretty

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.510
successful women who've
mentored you, right?

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.343
- Correct.

00:01:25.343 --> 00:01:26.730
- All right, Who or anybody you want to

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:27.690
talk about in particular?

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:28.990
Outside of my family,

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:30.350
I might have to go with probably,

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.940
you know, on the court Lisa Leslie.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.120
I mean, she was the first one that said

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.890
I want to be just like her.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:36.750
She's fierce.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:37.583
She's feminine.

00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:39.610
The first woman in the WNBA to dunk,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.480
broadcaster, entrepreneur
she got 50, 11 jobs.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.640
And so Lisa like even in my highs and lows

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.520
with injuries and setbacks,
she was always there for me.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.920
And then I guess in the classroom,

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.420
cause I'm low key a nerd,

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.990
- I am a nerd, so you're welcome.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:54.320
Welcome to Nerdville.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.340
- Here we go, I love it.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.600
Nerd nation, stand up.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.773
So I went to Stanford
university and when I was,

00:01:58.773 --> 00:02:00.340
- I am not that cool of a nerd,

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.350
you just, wow, that just happened.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.250
Okay, It's been a good time.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.080
Like, whoa, that's really cool.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.760
I wish I could be a Stanford nerd.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.830
I'm like your basic run of the mill nerd.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.110
- No you're honorary Stanford.
- Okay, cool.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:15.730
Yeah, no, I totally am with you now.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.850
So on my official visit to Stanford

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:18.683
when I was in high school,

00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:22.670
I had the nerdiest, the
nerdiest sort of official visit.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:23.970
You know, this is where
they try to recruit you

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.110
to go to the school.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.350
And so, you know, usually you
get those celebrities there.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.570
Like we had John Elway
which is a sports celebrity.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.670
Secretary Condoleezza Rice was there.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:33.503
- Wow.

00:02:33.503 --> 00:02:35.440
And then also Chief Justice John Roberts

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.380
was there at that pregame.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.370
So I was like nerd and I was like,

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.440
Oh my gosh this is the
place that I want to be.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.320
You know, going hard both,
you know, on the court.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:42.750
But then also off the court,

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:44.730
you know, aspirations academically.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.210
And so I go to Stanford
and we have this huge win.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.230
We beat UConn like one of their
80 or so game win streaks.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.150
And after the game we have
a lot of our, you know,

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.600
famous notable professors, alumni there.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.230
They come to our locker room.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.770
And you know, Secretary
Condoleezza Rice comes in.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:00.940
I'm like, Oh, she don't remember me.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:01.980
You know, I was just a little kid.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.180
I was baby.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.620
And so as we were leaving the locker room,

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:05.453
she was like, "Hey Chiney."

00:03:05.453 --> 00:03:06.797
I was like, Oh she remember me?"

00:03:07.862 --> 00:03:08.695
I was like, what?

00:03:08.695 --> 00:03:10.340
She's like "Chiney I heard,
I remember when we met

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.260
a couple of years ago,

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.670
you're interested in politics,

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:15.410
government affairs,
international relations.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:16.350
So why don't we meet?"

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:17.260
And I was like, Oh snap.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.409
So like next day I was like, okay,

00:03:18.409 --> 00:03:20.280
I'm going to follow up.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.863
So I went to her office and she-

00:03:21.863 --> 00:03:23.230
- Were you nervous?

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.280
- Was I nervous?
- God!

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.910
- Okay, So this is the funny part.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.057
So I go there and she says,

00:03:27.057 --> 00:03:29.127
"Chiney like have you been
raising your hand in class?"

00:03:29.127 --> 00:03:30.720
"Have you been participating?"

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.110
I was like, nah, like I'm nervous.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.770
This is Stanford like, I knew
in high school I was smart,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.180
but like Stanford smart,

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.870
is a different level of intimidation.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.370
So I go and she's like,
"Chiney you just like,

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.487
played UConn on national television."

00:03:43.487 --> 00:03:45.820
"And you could like play
basketball in front of millions

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.360
of people, but you can't
raise your hand in class?"

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.540
- Well, that's different.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.373
- It is different.

00:03:50.373 --> 00:03:51.500
But I was like, I get your point.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:52.730
- Okay. That's different.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:53.860
That's like I don't like speaking

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:55.460
in front of people like a speech,

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.150
but I can sing in front
of billions and be fine.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.650
- See it's a game mode.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:03:59.550
- Yeah.
- It's a game mode.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:00.660
- Yeah, you psych yourself, yeah.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:01.493
- Exactly.

00:04:01.493 --> 00:04:02.670
And so when she made that point

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.650
she started challenging me
to be as good as I can be

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.460
on the court, but also
understand that my energy

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.150
can also go off the court as well.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.510
And that's academics.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:13.180
And so as a freshman, it
sort of reset my mind.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:14.890
And so she's been a huge mentor for me.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.300
She actually was running

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.430
the international relations
department at Stanford.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:19.860
So I chose that major.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.990
And then she gave me my
diploma on graduation.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:24.892
And so she's my major advisor.
- Man.

00:04:24.892 --> 00:04:27.440
- What a beautiful life, you're only 29.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:28.540
Look at all the cool things you've done.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:30.530
And I think I want to point this out.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.590
Like the thing I love about sports ball,

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:35.580
that's what I call it, is it
creates so much opportunity.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:36.860
I have friends that
wouldn't have been able to

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.260
get into college, couldn't afford it,

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.250
but because of scholarships,

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.180
because of sports they were able to.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:45.040
So I think that, and then
like you said off, you know

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.580
academically speaking that helps getting

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.380
into such a great school and
then having that diploma.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.950
That's, there's just so
much opportunity there.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:51.783
- Parents are happy
- It's so cool.

00:04:51.783 --> 00:04:53.160
Yeah, I bet, yeah.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.240
They're not sad.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.493
I can promise you that.

